Given a valid labeling $w$, define $G_k = (V, E_k)$ where $E_k = \{uv : w(uv) \leq k\}$. In any triangle with labels $\{x, x, y\}$ ($y > x$): if $k \geq y$, all 3 edges are in $E_k$; if $x \leq k < y$, exactly 2 are; if $k < x$, none are. So **no triangle in $G_k$ has exactly one edge**.

A graph where no triangle has exactly one edge must be **complete multipartite**: if $uv \notin E_k$ and $vw \notin E_k$ but $uw \in E_k$, that's a triangle with exactly one edge — contradiction. So non-adjacency is transitive, making the complement a disjoint union of cliques.

Thus each $G_k$ is complete multipartite with partition $\mathcal{P}_k$. Since $G_1 \subseteq \cdots \subseteq G_m = K_n$, we get a refinement chain:
$$\mathcal{P}_0 = \{V\} \succ \mathcal{P}_1 \succ \cdots \succ \mathcal{P}_m = \{\text{singletons}\}.$$

The label $w(uv) = \min\{k : u, v \text{ separated in } \mathcal{P}_k\}$.

